# kasane-pdf compatibility-corpus storage configuration.
#
# The corpus lives in an S3-compatible object store (Cloudflare R2,
# AWS S3, MinIO, Backblaze B2, …). Nothing here is provider-specific —
# point the endpoint/bucket at *your* store. No credentials are kept
# in this file or anywhere in the repo; copy this to `.env` (which is
# git-ignored) or export the variables directly, and let credentials
# resolve through the standard AWS/boto3 provider chain.
#
#   cp compat/corpus.env.example .env   # then edit, or export inline

# ── Object-store location (REQUIRED for download/add) ──────────────
# Full S3 endpoint URL for your provider/region/account.
#   Cloudflare R2 : https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   AWS S3        : https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com   (or omit for the
#                   provider default via boto3)
#   MinIO         : http://localhost:9000
KASANE_CORPUS_ENDPOINT=https://YOUR-ENDPOINT.example.com

# Bucket holding the corpus objects.
KASANE_CORPUS_BUCKET=your-corpus-bucket

# Object-key prefix under which PDFs are stored (default "pdfs").
# Layout is  <prefix>/<category>/<sha256>.pdf
#KASANE_CORPUS_PREFIX=pdfs

# ── Credentials (NOT stored here) ──────────────────────────────────
# Use the standard AWS provider chain instead of editing this file:
#   export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=…
#   export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=…
# or an AWS profile / IAM role / instance metadata:
#   export AWS_PROFILE=corpus
#   export AWS_REGION=auto            # R2 uses "auto"; S3 uses a region

# ── Alternative: skip the store entirely ───────────────────────────
# The compat runner reads a plain directory of PDFs. To build one from
# files you already have (matched to the manifest by sha256):
#   .venv-corpus/bin/python compat/fetch-corpus.py seed \
#       --from /path/to/my/pdfs --dir compat/corpus
# …or just point the runner at any directory:  --corpus /path/to/pdfs
